## std/numerus.pengu — the number: universal numeric methods over the `Num`
## concept.
##
## `numerus` dota de métodos a **todos** los primitivos numéricos de
## PenguScript (`int`, `i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`, `usize`, `isize`, `byte`,
## `float`, `f32`, `f64`, `double`) mediante un único bloque
## `enchanting shard T where T: Num and T: Ordo and T: Par:`. El compilador
## monomorfiza cada método por tipo concreto en tiempo de compilación
## (`int_abs`, `f64_clamp`, `u32_signum`, …), así que no hay vtables, ni
## boxing, ni coste en runtime.
##
## ## `Num` y las comparaciones
## En esta versión del lenguaje `Num` concede aritmética (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`,
## unario `-`) pero **no** orden ni igualdad: ésos son los conceptos
## ortogonales `Ordo` (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) y `Par` (`==`, `!=`). Todo
## primitivo numérico satisface los tres, así que el bound compuesto sigue
## cubriendo exactamente los mismos 11 tipos canónicos con una sola
## declaración (LANGUAGE.md §11.3).
##
## ## Delegación
## Este módulo es la **fuente de verdad** de la aritmética escalar. Las
## funciones escalares de `std.arithmancy` (`abs`/`abs_i`, `min`/`min_i`,
## `max`/`max_i`, `clamp`/`clamp_i`, `sign`/`sign_i`, `to_int_round`/
## `to_int_floor`/`to_int_ceil`) son ahora wrappers de una línea sobre estos
## métodos.
##
## Cuatro de ellos **no** se delegan desde `arithmancy`, y no por descuido:
## `truncate`, `floor`, `ceil` y `round` pasan por `int` para redondear
## (`self to int`), así que solo son exactos mientras `|self|` quepa en `int`.
## Las versiones de `arithmancy` son las de C (`floorf`, `round`, …) y cubren
## todo el dominio de `float`/`f64`; cambiar una por otra alteraría resultados
## fuera de ese rango. `## Precondiciones` de cada método lo documenta.
##
## ## Alcance
## `numerus` cubre lo que tiene sentido para **cualquier** tipo numérico
## (enteros y flotantes). Los métodos exclusivos de enteros (`is_even`,
## `is_odd`, `gcd`, `lcm`, `bit_count`) viven en `std.integrum`; los
## exclusivos de flotantes (`sqrt`, `is_nan`, `approx_eq`, `sin`, `cos`) en
## `std.fractio`. Las conversiones sin método (`x to int`) siguen siendo la
## vía idiomática para cambiar de tipo; `to_f64`/`to_i64` sólo existen por
## comodidad encadenable.
##
## ## Precondiciones
## - `clamp with lo, hi` e `is_between with lo, hi` exigen `lo <= hi`. No
##   paniquean (evitar importar `std.spark` sólo por eso); con `lo > hi` el
##   resultado es el del algoritmo, que para `is_between` es siempre `false`.
## - `pow_int with exp` exige `exp != INT_MIN` (la negación del exponente
##   desbordaría). Los exponentes negativos sobre tipos enteros truncan hacia
##   cero: `2.pow_int with -3` es `0`, no `0.125`.
##
## ## Memoria
## Ningún método de este módulo asigna memoria del heap. No hay nada que
## liberar con `banish`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## import std.numerus
##
## weave main into int:
##     var edad as int is 200
##     var corregida as int is calling edad.clamp with 0, 120
##     var temp as f64 is -3.7
##     var abs_temp as f64 is calling temp.abs
##     return 0
## ```

import std.ward

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module Version (LANGUAGE.md §19.0: every hand-written std module exports one)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Numerus module version.
const NUMERUS_VERSION as string is "2.14.0"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Universal numeric methods (one body, one C function per concrete receiver)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enchanting shard T where T: Num and T: Ordo and T: Par:

    # ─── Valor absoluto y signo ──────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve el valor absoluto de `self`.
    ##
    ## Para tipos sin signo (`u8`–`u64`, `usize`, `byte`) es la identidad:
    ## la condición `self < donum T` nunca se cumple.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v1 as int is (-5)
    ## calling v1.abs              # 5
    ## var v2 as f64 is 3.5
    ## calling v2.abs               # 3.5
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave abs into T:
        if self < donum T:
            return -self
        return self

    ## Devuelve `-1`, `0` o `1` según el signo de `self`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v3 as int is (-42)
    ## calling v3.signum    # -1
    ## var v4 as int is 0
    ## calling v4.signum        # 0
    ## var v5 as f64 is 7.5
    ## calling v5.signum      # 1.0
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave signum into T:
        if self > donum T:
            return (1 to T)
        if self < donum T:
            return (0 - (1 to T)) to T
        return donum T

    ## Devuelve `-self`. Equivalente al menos unario, pero encadenable.
    ##
    ## En tipos sin signo el resultado envuelve (aritmética modular de C).
    @mutates
    weave negated into T:
        return -self

    # ─── Comparación y restricción ───────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve el menor de `self` y `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v6 as int is 3
    ## calling v6.min_with with 7      # 3
    ## var v7 as f64 is 3.0
    ## calling v7.min_with with 2.5  # 2.5
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave min_with with other as T into T:
        if self < other:
            return self
        return other

    ## Devuelve el mayor de `self` y `other`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v8 as int is 3
    ## calling v8.max_with with 7      # 7
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave max_with with other as T into T:
        if self > other:
            return self
        return other

    ## Restringe `self` al rango cerrado `[lo, hi]`.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `lo <= hi`. El comportamiento es indefinido si se viola.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v9 as int is 200
    ## calling v9.clamp with 0, 120    # 120
    ## var v10 as int is (-5)
    ## calling v10.clamp with 0, 10    # 0
    ## var v11 as int is 7
    ## calling v11.clamp with 0, 10       # 7
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave clamp with lo as T, hi as T into T:
        if self < lo:
            return lo
        if self > hi:
            return hi
        return self

    ## Devuelve `true` si `lo <= self <= hi` (rango cerrado).
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `lo <= hi`. Con `lo > hi` devuelve siempre `false`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v12 as int is 5
    ## calling v12.is_between with 0, 10    # true
    ## var v13 as int is 15
    ## calling v13.is_between with 0, 10   # false
    ## ```
    weave is_between with lo as T, hi as T into bool:
        return self >= lo and self <= hi

    # ─── Predicados ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self == donum T` (el cero de `T`).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v14 as int is 0
    ## calling v14.is_zero      # true
    ## var v15 as f64 is 0.0
    ## calling v15.is_zero    # true
    ## ```
    weave is_zero into bool:
        return self == donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self > 0`. Siempre `false` para tipos sin signo
    ## cuando `self == 0`.
    weave is_positive into bool:
        return self > donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self < 0`. Siempre `false` para tipos sin signo.
    weave is_negative into bool:
        return self < donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self >= 0`. Siempre `true` para tipos sin signo.
    weave is_non_negative into bool:
        return self >= donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self <= 0`.
    weave is_non_positive into bool:
        return self <= donum T

    # ─── Transformaciones aritméticas ────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `self + self` (el doble de `self`).
    ##
    ## El nombre es `twice` y no `double` porque `double` es un tipo.
    @mutates
    weave twice into T:
        return self + self

    ## Devuelve `self / 2`.
    ##
    ## En tipos enteros la división trunca hacia cero: `5.half` es `2` y
    ## `(-5).half` es `-2`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v16 as int is 10
    ## calling v16.half     # 5
    ## var v17 as f64 is 2.5
    ## calling v17.half    # 1.25
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave half into T:
        return self / (2 to T)

    ## Devuelve `self * self`.
    @mutates
    weave square into T:
        return self * self

    ## Devuelve `self * self * self`.
    @mutates
    weave cube into T:
        return self * self * self

    ## Eleva `self` a un exponente entero por cuadrados.
    ##
    ## ## Comportamiento por signo del exponente
    ## - `exp == 0` → `1` (de tipo `T`).
    ## - `exp > 0` → potenciación por cuadrados.
    ## - `exp < 0` → `(1 to T) / self.pow_int with (0 - exp)`. **En tipos
    ##   enteros esto trunca hacia cero**: `2.pow_int with -3` es `0`.
    ##   Convierte a `f64` primero (`(self to f64).pow_int with exp`) si
    ##   necesitas un resultado fraccionario.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `exp != INT_MIN`: negar `INT_MIN` desborda. El exponente se evalúa en
    ## `int`, con independencia de `T`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v18 as int is 2
    ## calling v18.pow_int with 10       # 1024
    ## var v19 as f64 is 2.0
    ## calling v19.pow_int with 10     # 1024.0
    ## var v20 as f64 is 2.0
    ## calling v20.pow_int with -1     # 0.5
    ## ```
    @mutates
    weave pow_int with exp as int into T:
        if exp < 0:
            return (1 to T) / (calling self.get_pow_int with (0 - exp))
        var r as T is (1 to T)
        var base as T is self
        var e as int is exp
        while e > 0:
            if (e % 2) == 1:
                set r is r * base
            set base is base * base
            set e is e / 2
        return r

    # ─── Redondeo ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # Los cuatro devuelven `T`, igual que `Math.floor` (Java),
    # `Double.floor()` (Kotlin) y `f64::floor` (Rust). Para tipos enteros son
    # la identidad. La implementación pasa por `int` (truncamiento hacia
    # cero) y corrige; el checker acepta el cast genérico `T to int` y la
    # vuelta `int to T`.

    ## Trunca hacia cero (el mayor entero con |resultado| ≤ |self|).
    ##
    ## Para tipos enteros es la identidad.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## En flotantes el truncado pasa por `int`, así que `|self|` debe caber en
    ## `int`; fuera de ese rango usa `arithmancy.trunc`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v21 as f64 is 2.7
    ## calling v21.truncate      # 2.0
    ## var v22 as f64 is (-2.7)
    ## calling v22.truncate   # -2.0
    ## ```
    # NOTE: `arithmancy.trunc` is the full-domain C `trunc`; this one rounds
    # through `int`, so it is only exact while `|self|` fits in `int`.
    @mutates
    weave truncate into T:
        return (self to int) to T

    ## Redondea hacia menos infinito (el mayor entero ≤ `self`).
    ##
    ## Para tipos enteros es la identidad.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## En flotantes el redondeo pasa por `int`, así que `|self|` debe caber en
    ## `int`. Fuera de ese rango (`2.7e20.floor`) el resultado no está
    ## definido; usa `arithmancy.floor` (el `floor` de C) para el dominio
    ## completo de `float`/`f64`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v23 as f64 is 2.7
    ## calling v23.floor         # 2.0
    ## var v24 as f64 is (-2.7)
    ## calling v24.floor      # -3.0
    ## ```
    # NOTE: `arithmancy.floor` is the full-domain C `floor`; this one rounds
    # through `int`, so it is only exact while `|self|` fits in `int`.
    @mutates
    weave floor into T:
        let t as int is self to int
        if (t to T) > self:
            return ((t - 1) to T)
        return (t to T)

    ## Redondea hacia más infinito (el menor entero ≥ `self`).
    ##
    ## Para tipos enteros es la identidad.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## En flotantes el redondeo pasa por `int`, así que `|self|` debe caber en
    ## `int`; fuera de ese rango usa `arithmancy.ceil`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v25 as f64 is 2.3
    ## calling v25.ceil          # 3.0
    ## var v26 as f64 is (-2.3)
    ## calling v26.ceil       # -2.0
    ## ```
    # NOTE: `arithmancy.ceil` is the full-domain C `ceil`; this one rounds
    # through `int`, so it is only exact while `|self|` fits in `int`.
    @mutates
    weave ceil into T:
        let t as int is self to int
        if (t to T) < self:
            return ((t + 1) to T)
        return (t to T)

    ## Redondea al entero más cercano con la convención **half away from
    ## zero**: `2.5` → `3.0`, `(-2.5)` → `-3.0`, `2.4` → `2.0`.
    ##
    ## Para tipos enteros es la identidad (el `half` entero es `0`).
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## En flotantes el redondeo pasa por `int`, así que `|self|` debe caber en
    ## `int`; fuera de ese rango usa `arithmancy.round`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v27 as f64 is 3.5
    ## calling v27.round     # 4.0
    ## var v28 as f64 is 2.4
    ## calling v28.round     # 2.0
    ## ```
    # NOTE: `arithmancy.round` is the full-domain C `round`; this one rounds
    # through `int`, so it is only exact while `|self|` fits in `int`.
    @mutates
    weave round into T:
        let half as T is (1 to T) / (2 to T)
        if self < donum T:
            return (0 - (((0 - self) + half) to int)) to T
        return ((self + half) to int) to T

    # ─── Conversiones ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # El lenguaje ya tiene `(x to T)`; estos dos métodos existen sólo para
    # encadenar (`x.abs.to_f64`) y son siempre totales: `to_i64` trunca hacia
    # cero en flotantes y envuelve en `u64` fuera de rango (documentado).

    ## Convierte a `f64`. Siempre seguro (ensanchamiento para enteros y
    ## `f32`).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v29 as int is 42
    ## calling v29.to_f64       # 42.0
    ## var v30 as f64 is 2.5
    ## calling v30.to_f64      # 2.5
    ## ```
    weave to_f64 into f64:
        return self to f64

    ## Convierte a `i64`. En flotantes trunca hacia cero; en `u64` los
    ## valores mayores que `2^63 - 1` son indefinidos (comportamiento del
    ## cast del lenguaje).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var v31 as f64 is 2.9
    ## calling v31.to_i64      # 2
    ## var v32 as f64 is (-2.9)
    ## calling v32.to_i64   # -2
    ## ```
    weave to_i64 into i64:
        return self to i64

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

test "abs leaves positives untouched":
    var x as int is 5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_abs), 5

test "abs flips negatives":
    var x as int is -3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_abs), 3

test "abs is the identity at zero":
    var x as int is 0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_abs), 0

test "abs works for wide and unsigned integers":
    var a as i64 is -9000000
    var b as u32 is 7
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_abs to int), 9000000
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_abs), 7

test "abs works for every float width":
    var a as f64 is -3.5
    var b as f32 is -2.5
    var c as float is -1.5
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.get_abs) == 3.5
    calling ward.assert_true with ((calling b.get_abs to f64)) == 2.5
    calling ward.assert_true with ((calling c.get_abs to f64)) == 1.5

test "signum reports the sign of each type":
    var a as int is -42
    var b as int is 0
    var c as f64 is 7.5
    var d as u32 is 9
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_signum), -1
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_signum), 0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling c.get_signum) == 1.0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling d.get_signum), 1

test "negated inverts the value":
    var a as int is 12
    var b as int is -12
    var c as int is 0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_negated), -12
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_negated), 12
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_negated), 0

test "min_with picks the smaller operand":
    var x as int is 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_min_with with 7), 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_min_with with 2), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_min_with with 3), 3

test "max_with picks the larger operand":
    var x as int is 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_max_with with 7), 7
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_max_with with 2), 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_max_with with 3), 3

test "clamp restricts below, inside and above":
    var x as int is 200
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_clamp with 0, 120), 120
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_clamp with 0, 500), 200
    var y as int is -5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling y.get_clamp with 0, 10), 0

test "clamp keeps the inclusive boundaries":
    var x as int is 10
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_clamp with 0, 10), 10
    var z as int is 0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling z.get_clamp with 0, 10), 0

test "clamp works on floats":
    var x as f64 is 145.7
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling x.get_clamp with 0.0, 100.0) == 100.0
    var y as f64 is -3.0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling y.get_clamp with 0.0, 100.0) == 0.0

test "is_between accepts the closed range only":
    var x as int is 5
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling x.is_between with 0, 10)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling x.is_between with 6, 10)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling x.is_between with 0, 4)

test "is_between includes both edges":
    var lo as int is 0
    var hi as int is 10
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling lo.is_between with 0, 10)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling hi.is_between with 0, 10)

test "is_zero is true only at zero":
    var a as int is 0
    var b as int is 1
    var c as int is -1
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.is_zero)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling b.is_zero)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling c.is_zero)

test "is_positive distinguishes positive from zero and negative":
    var a as int is 7
    var b as int is 0
    var c as int is -7
    var d as u8 is 0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.is_positive)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling b.is_positive)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling c.is_positive)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling d.is_positive)

test "is_negative is false for unsigned types":
    var a as int is -7
    var b as int is 0
    var c as u64 is 5
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.is_negative)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling b.is_negative)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling c.is_negative)

test "is_non_negative is true for unsigned types":
    var a as int is -1
    var b as int is 0
    var c as int is 1
    var d as u16 is 0
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling a.is_non_negative)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling b.is_non_negative)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling c.is_non_negative)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling d.is_non_negative)

test "is_non_positive mirrors non-negative":
    var a as int is -1
    var b as int is 0
    var c as int is 1
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.is_non_positive)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling b.is_non_positive)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling c.is_non_positive)

test "twice doubles without losing the type":
    var a as int is 21
    var b as int is -4
    var c as f64 is 2.5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_twice), 42
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_twice), -8
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling c.get_twice) == 5.0

test "half truncates toward zero for integers":
    var a as int is 10
    var b as int is 5
    var c as int is -5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_half), 5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_half), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_half), -2

test "half is exact for floats":
    var a as f64 is 2.5
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.get_half) == 1.25

test "square keeps the sign of the input":
    var a as int is 4
    var b as int is -4
    var c as int is 0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_square), 16
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_square), 16
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_square), 0

test "cube keeps the sign of the input":
    var a as int is 3
    var b as int is -3
    var c as int is 0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_cube), 27
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_cube), -27
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_cube), 0

test "pow_int with a zero exponent is one":
    var x as int is 7
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_pow_int with 0), 1

test "pow_int with a positive exponent":
    var x as int is 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_pow_int with 10), 1024
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_pow_int with 1), 2

test "pow_int truncates for integer negative exponents":
    var x as int is 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_pow_int with -3), 0

test "pow_int is fractional for float negative exponents":
    var x as f64 is 2.0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling x.get_pow_int with -1) == 0.5

test "truncate drops the fractional part toward zero":
    var a as f64 is 2.7
    var b as f64 is -2.7
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_truncate to int), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_truncate to int), -2

test "truncate is the identity for integers":
    var x as int is 42
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_truncate), 42

test "floor rounds toward negative infinity":
    var a as f64 is 2.7
    var b as f64 is -2.7
    var c as f64 is 2.0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_floor to int), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_floor to int), -3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_floor to int), 2

test "ceil rounds toward positive infinity":
    var a as f64 is 2.3
    var b as f64 is -2.3
    var c as f64 is 2.0
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_ceil to int), 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_ceil to int), -2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_ceil to int), 2

test "round uses half away from zero":
    var a as f64 is 2.4
    var b as f64 is 2.5
    var c as f64 is 2.6
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_round to int), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_round to int), 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_round to int), 3

test "round is symmetric for negatives":
    var a as f64 is -2.4
    var b as f64 is -2.5
    var c as f64 is -2.6
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.get_round to int), -2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.get_round to int), -3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.get_round to int), -3

test "round is the identity for integers":
    var x as int is 5
    var y as int is -5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling x.get_round), 5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling y.get_round), -5

test "to_f64 widens integers and floats":
    var a as int is 42
    var b as i64 is -7
    var c as f32 is 2.5
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling a.to_f64) == 42.0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling b.to_f64) == -7.0
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling c.to_f64) == 2.5

test "to_i64 truncates floats toward zero":
    var a as f64 is 2.9
    var b as f64 is -2.9
    var c as int is 12
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling a.to_i64 to int), 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling b.to_i64 to int), -2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (calling c.to_i64 to int), 12

test "methods chain across the module":
    var raw as f64 is -145.7
    var magnitude as f64 is calling raw.get_abs
    var clamped as f64 is calling magnitude.get_clamp with 0.0, 100.0
    var rounded as int is calling clamped.get_round to int
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with rounded, 100

# --- In-place mutation vs the pure `get_` form -------------------------------
#
# Every method above is `@mutates`: `calling x.abs` rewrites `x`, while
# `calling x.get_abs` returns the value and leaves `x` alone.

test "abs mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is -3
    calling x.abs
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 3
test "get_abs returns without mutating":
    var x as int is -3
    var y as int is calling x.get_abs
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, -3
test "signum mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is -5
    calling x.signum
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, -1
test "get_signum returns without mutating":
    var x as int is -5
    var y as int is calling x.get_signum
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, -1
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, -5
test "negated mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 7
    calling x.negated
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, -7
test "get_negated returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 7
    var y as int is calling x.get_negated
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, -7
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 7
test "min_with mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 10
    calling x.min_with with 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 3
test "get_min_with returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 10
    var y as int is calling x.get_min_with with 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 10
test "max_with mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 10
    calling x.max_with with 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 10
test "get_max_with returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 10
    var y as int is calling x.get_max_with with 3
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 10
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 10
test "clamp mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 150
    calling x.clamp with 0, 100
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 100
test "get_clamp returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 150
    var y as int is calling x.get_clamp with 0, 100
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 100
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 150
test "twice mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 4
    calling x.twice
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 8
test "get_twice returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 4
    var y as int is calling x.get_twice
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 8
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 4
test "half mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 9
    calling x.half
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 4
test "get_half returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 9
    var y as int is calling x.get_half
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 4
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 9
test "square mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 3
    calling x.square
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 9
test "get_square returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 3
    var y as int is calling x.get_square
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 9
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 3
test "cube mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 2
    calling x.cube
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 8
test "get_cube returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 2
    var y as int is calling x.get_cube
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 8
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 2
test "pow_int mutates the receiver":
    var x as int is 2
    calling x.pow_int with 5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 32
test "get_pow_int returns without mutating":
    var x as int is 2
    var y as int is calling x.get_pow_int with 5
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with y, 32
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 2
test "truncate mutates the receiver":
    var f as float is 3.7
    calling f.truncate
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.0
test "get_truncate returns without mutating":
    var f as float is 3.7
    var g as float is calling f.get_truncate
    calling ward.assert_true with g == 3.0
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.7
test "floor mutates the receiver":
    var f as float is 3.7
    calling f.floor
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.0
test "get_floor returns without mutating":
    var f as float is 3.7
    var g as float is calling f.get_floor
    calling ward.assert_true with g == 3.0
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.7
test "ceil mutates the receiver":
    var f as float is 3.2
    calling f.ceil
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 4.0
test "get_ceil returns without mutating":
    var f as float is 3.2
    var g as float is calling f.get_ceil
    calling ward.assert_true with g == 4.0
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.2
test "round mutates the receiver":
    var f as float is 3.5
    calling f.round
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 4.0
test "get_round returns without mutating":
    var f as float is 3.5
    var g as float is calling f.get_round
    calling ward.assert_true with g == 4.0
    calling ward.assert_true with f == 3.5
test "chained mutations on one receiver":
    var x as int is -50
    calling x.clamp with -30, 30
    calling x.abs
    calling x.twice
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with x, 60
